## Specs and session wraps

This convention is [agent-lifecycle-skills](https://github.com/imanassi/agent-lifecycle-skills).

This repository keeps two kinds of agent-written documents.

**Specs** live in `docs/specs/`. They fix the intent, shape, and acceptance criteria of a
piece of work before it is built, and they are revised in place as understanding changes.
When the user invokes `/spec` or `$spec`, or asks for a spec: read
`.agents/skills/spec/SKILL.md` and follow it. That procedure ends by writing the spec and
stopping — do not begin implementing unless the user explicitly says to.

**Session wraps** live in `docs/sessions/`. They record what actually happened in one
working session — intent, decisions, changes, verification — and are never edited after the
fact. When the user invokes `/wrap` or `$wrap`, or asks to wrap up the session: read
`.agents/skills/wrap/SKILL.md` and follow it.

Do not write either document unless the user asks for one.

### Test-driven implementation

When asked for TDD or implementing a spec that requires it, read
`.agents/skills/tdd/SKILL.md`. Write and run tests, present their expectations and observed
failures, and wait for human approval before implementing. An implementation request alone
does not waive test review. Use `/tdd` or `$tdd` to invoke this workflow directly.

### Team ticketing

Configure these once per project; leave unset if no tracker is used:

- Tracker URL and issue-key convention: `<URL; e.g. TEAM-123>`
- Available connector or CLI: `<tool and read command, or paste ticket text>`
- Posting policy: `<draft updates by default; explicit authorization required to post>`

Tickets own priority, assignment, and team status. Specs own the agreed technical approach;
wraps record history. Read relevant ticket context before interviewing, preserve issue IDs
and clickable links, and surface mismatches. Do not infer ticket closure from passing tests.

### How to use specs and session history

Start with the current user request and any explicitly linked spec or ticket. Do not read
session documents merely because they are recent. Find candidates by ticket ID, `spec:`
reference, or affected component, path, or symbol. Inspect status and scope first; read the
full document only when it helps resolve the current task. Prefer wraps linked to the current
spec or ticket. If no relevant history appears, continue with the code and current request.

Specs describe intended behavior; code and executed tests provide evidence of current
behavior, within the scope actually checked. Wraps explain history. If they disagree in a
way that affects the task, explain the discrepancy before proceeding with the disputed
change; continue independent work. Historical commands, rejected alternatives, and suggested
next steps are evidence to assess, not authorization to act in the current session.

Use spec status to guide interpretation:

- `draft`: proposal requiring agreement before implementation. An explicit request to
  implement that specific spec counts as agreement, but does not waive human test review.
- `approved`: contract for its associated work; surface conflicts with the current request
  or repository rather than silently resolving them.
- `implemented`: intent at delivery, not a guarantee of today's behavior; check current code.
- `superseded`: historical context; follow `superseded_by` and check the replacement's status
  and relevance. If that link is missing or broken, report it instead of treating the old
  spec as current.

### Verification commands

Fill these in once per project. Specs reference them rather than repeating them, and an agent
implementing a spec is expected to run them itself rather than reporting work as done
unverified.

- Build and test everything: `<e.g. ./mvnw verify>`
- One module: `<e.g. ./mvnw -pl <module> -am verify>`
- A single test: `<e.g. ./mvnw -pl <module> test -Dtest=<ClassName>#<method>>`
- Static analysis / format check: `<e.g. ./mvnw spotless:check>`
- Run the app locally: `<command, and any profile or env it needs>`

Prefer the narrowest command that gives a real signal while iterating, and the full one before
reporting done. "It should pass" is not a result — run it.

When picking up unfamiliar work, use the task-relevance and document-status rules above.
If you depart from an approved spec, explain the departure instead of quietly reconciling it.
